<section class="phero"><div class="phero-img"><img src="/assets/img/photos/storm-over-homes.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/storm-over-homes-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/storm-over-homes.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-calculators/">Calculators</a></li><li aria-current="page">Percentage Deductible Calculator</li></ol></nav><p class="eyebrow on-dark">Free calculator · runs in your browser</p><h1>Wind and Hail Percentage Deductible Calculator for Kentucky Homes</h1><p class="lede">A 2% deductible sounds small until you multiply it by your dwelling limit. Compare a percentage deductible with a flat one for the same loss. Everything runs in your browser and nothing is stored.</p></div></section>
<section class="sec toolsec"><div class="wrap">
<div class="calc" data-tool="deduct">
  <div class="calc-bar"><h2 class="calc-t">Percentage deductible calculator</h2><span>Wind/hail % vs. flat</span></div>
  <div class="calc-grid">
    <div class="calc-in">
      <div><label for="w-a">Dwelling coverage — Coverage A ($)</label><input id="w-a" name="cova" inputmode="decimal" value="312000"><small>From your declarations page.</small></div>
      <div class="pair">
        <div><label for="w-p">Wind/hail deductible</label><select id="w-p" name="pct"><option value="1" selected>1%</option><option value="2">2%</option><option value="3">3%</option><option value="5">5%</option></select></div>
        <div><label for="w-f">All-other-perils deductible ($)</label><input id="w-f" name="flat" inputmode="decimal" value="1000"></div>
      </div>
      <div><label for="w-l">Estimated storm damage ($)</label><input id="w-l" name="loss" inputmode="decimal" value="14800"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="calc-out" aria-live="polite">
      <p class="kpi"><small>Your percentage deductible in dollars</small><b data-o="pd">—</b></p>
      <div class="stack two" aria-hidden="true"><span class="s3"></span><span class="s1"></span></div>
      <ul class="key"><li><i class="s3"></i>You pay</li><li><i class="s1"></i>Policy pays</li></ul>
      <dl class="rows"><div><dt>Payment with the % deductible</dt><dd data-o="pdpay">—</dd></div><div><dt>Payment if the flat deductible applied</dt><dd data-o="flatpay">—</dd></div><div><dt>Extra out of pocket</dt><dd data-o="diff">—</dd></div><div><dt>Deductible as a share of this loss</dt><dd data-o="share">—</dd></div><div><dt>Break-even loss size</dt><dd data-o="breakeven">—</dd></div></dl>
      <p class="say" data-o="below">Results update as you type.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<p class="fine">For planning only. Results depend on what you enter and on your policy’s terms. They are not a quote, an appraisal or legal advice. Nothing you type leaves your browser.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><h2>How to use the calculator</h2>
<p>Find your Coverage A (dwelling) limit and your wind/hail deductible percentage on the declarations page. Enter both, plus your flat all-other-perils deductible and the estimated size of the loss. The tool shows the percentage deductible in dollars, what the insurer would pay under each deductible, the extra you pay out of pocket because of the percentage, the deductible as a share of the loss, and the break-even loss size.</p>
<h2>The math in plain words</h2>
<p>A percentage deductible is calculated on the dwelling limit, not on the size of the loss. So the dollar deductible is Coverage A times the percentage. Payment under each option is the loss minus that deductible, never less than zero. The extra out-of-pocket is the difference between the two deductibles, up to the size of the loss. The <strong>break-even loss size</strong> is simply the dollar deductible: any covered loss below it pays nothing.</p>
<h3>An illustrative example</h3>
<p>Illustrative figures only: a home with $300,000 of Coverage A and a 2% wind/hail deductible has a $6,000 deductible. On a $14,000 hail claim, the insurer pays $8,000. With a flat $1,000 deductible it would pay $13,000, so the percentage costs $5,000 more. A $5,500 claim would pay nothing.</p>
<h2>What the result does and doesn’t mean</h2>
<p>The calculator assumes the deductible applies to the whole loss. Real policies differ: some apply the percentage only to named storm, hail or windstorm losses, some set a minimum dollar amount, and some apply it per occurrence across several buildings. The deductible is also subtracted from what the loss is actually worth, so if the estimate is short, the deductible eats a larger share of a smaller number. Check the exact endorsement wording before relying on the result.</p>
<h2>Kentucky context</h2>
<p>Percentage wind and hail deductibles show up on many Kentucky policies, especially after rounds of spring hail across the Bluegrass and western Kentucky. Because the deductible is fixed, every dollar the estimate is low comes straight out of your recovery. If the carrier’s number barely clears the deductible, it is worth checking the scope before accepting it; our <a href="/insurance-appraisal/tornado-wind-appraisal/">tornado and wind appraisal</a> page explains how pricing disputes on storm claims are handled, and the <a href="/appraisal-calculators/appraisal-payoff-estimator/">appraisal payoff estimator</a> helps you weigh whether appraisal is worth it.</p>
<h2>Questions about the percentage deductible calculator</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is a percentage deductible based on the loss or the dwelling limit?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>On most policies it is based on the Coverage A dwelling limit shown on the declarations page, not on the size of the loss. Check your endorsement for the exact wording.</p></details><details><summary>Can appraisal change my deductible?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>No. Appraisal sets the amount of loss. It can change the number the deductible is subtracted from, but not the deductible itself.</p></details><details><summary>Is my policy information stored?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>No. The calculation runs in your browser and nothing is saved or sent to us.</p></details></div>
